    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "AIDS",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "The National Institutes of Health (NIH) invited 200 researchers and clinicians to a meeting entitled \"Gender and Pain: A Focus on How Pain Impacts Women Differently Than Men.\" The interdisciplinary program sifted through the different ways of perceiving and treating pain, and identified areas where further research is needed. The traditional view of pain is that it is a response to a stimulus, but views were presented that the body sometimes creates the pain in an effort to remain healthy, i.e., to force the individual to seek medical care. Women generally have a lower pain threshold than men, which may be linked to serotonin labeling in the spinal cords of women and men. The role of pain receptors and vagus nerves are reviewed, as well as the effects of analgesia on both genders. Data submitted to the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for drug approvals frequently do not address the drug's effect on pain. Data should include gender, age, and patient's size in terms of both height and weight to help identify problems related to dosing. However, the FDA has shown little inclination to set standards for the uniform submission of data."
    }
